  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1223 AM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nA strong 594 dm ridge of high pressure aloft will build over the \nlower Ohio Valley this weekend into early next week, providing \nabundant sunshine and tranquil weather. Weak flow in the column will \ninhibit moisture transport from the south, allowing for dewpoints to \nmix down into the lower 60s again today due to a deep dry layer \nabove 800 mb. As a weak area of high pressure at the sfc builds east \non Sunday, a modest increase in the pressure gradient occurs that \nwill allow for light southerly return flow into next week. Despite a \nsubtle increase in moisture, dewpoints struggle to rise above 70 \ndegrees, supporting daily NBM highs in the low to mid 90s. The dry \nheat yields a 70-90% chance on the LREF of heat index values \nexceeding 100 degrees from Tuesday through the remainder of the week \nthat will cause a major to extreme heat risk.\n\nThere are signs the ridge begins to breakdown and migrate west next \nweekend as a frontal boundary washes out to the north. Increasing \nrain chances becomes more probable and may bring some limited \nrelief; however, NBM PoPs only peak at a 15-30% chance at best on \nFriday to end the week. Model ensembles including the ENS are \nmore optimistic in seeing some light rain chances over \nnortheast portions of the region with mid-level energy moving\ndownstream from the Plains.",
